# Settings for running CareerBot with Docker Compose (compose.yaml). Copy this file to .env and fill it in.
# Never commit .env: it holds keys.
#
# After `docker compose up -d`, read the setup code with `docker compose logs convex-setup`, open SITE_URL and
# create your account with it: the first account owns this copy and adds everyone else (Settings, Account). Lost the
# code? docker compose run --rm convex-setup new-setup-code

# ── Version ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which CareerBot release to run. Unset: the release this compose.yaml came with. A version such as 0.8.0 stays on it
# until you change it here; `latest` moves to the newest release on each `docker compose pull`. Before changing it,
# back up and read the release notes: https://careerbot.dev/changelog
# CAREERBOT_VERSION=

# ── Your site ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where people open CareerBot, as the browser sees it. On your own computer: http://localhost:3000. On a server with a
# domain: its https address (https://careerbot.example.com), with your proxy or tunnel sending it to WEB_PORT.
SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── Convex: pick one ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A) Convex's cloud (free plan). Create a project at https://dashboard.convex.dev, then:
#    NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL: Settings > URL & Deploy Key, the deployment URL (https://….convex.cloud)
#    CONVEX_DEPLOY_KEY: Settings > URL & Deploy Key, Generate Production Deploy Key
#    CONVEX_SITE_ORIGIN isn't needed: Google and GitHub send people back to https://….convex.site.
#    Uncomment the two lines below and comment out or delete B's four lines.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL=https://your-deployment.convex.cloud
# CONVEX_DEPLOY_KEY=prod:your-deployment|…

# B) Self-hosted Convex, run by this compose file on this computer. Its data stays in the convex-data Docker volume.
#    On a server with a domain, NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL and CONVEX_SITE_ORIGIN are two https addresses of their own
#    (https://convex.example.com, https://convex-site.example.com) that your proxy sends to CONVEX_PORT and
#    CONVEX_SITE_PORT. CONVEX_SELF_HOSTED_URL stays as it is: the setup step reaches Convex inside compose.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=self-hosted
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL=http://localhost:3210
CONVEX_SITE_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3211
CONVEX_SELF_HOSTED_URL=http://convex-backend:3210

# ── Signing in ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# People sign in with a username and password: the owner's account is made with the setup code, and the owner adds
# everyone else in Settings, Account. Google and GitHub are optional; the sign-in screen offers them once they're set.
#
# Who may sign in with Google or GitHub: their emails, comma-separated. Empty: nobody (password accounts don't need it).
# Changing the list takes effect after `docker compose up -d`; to empty it again, delete SIGNUP_ALLOWLIST in the
# Convex dashboard's environment variables (an empty value here leaves the one there as it is).
SIGNUP_ALLOWLIST=
#
# Each of Google and GitHub needs an OAuth app whose callback URL is your Convex HTTP actions URL plus
# /api/auth/callback/google (or /github):
#   self-hosted on this computer: http://localhost:3211/api/auth/callback/google
#   self-hosted on a server:      CONVEX_SITE_ORIGIN/api/auth/callback/google (https://convex-site.example.com/…)
#   Convex's cloud:               https://your-deployment.convex.site/api/auth/callback/google
# Google: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials (OAuth client ID, Web application)
# GitHub: https://github.com/settings/developers (New OAuth App; its homepage URL is SITE_URL)
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=
AUTH_GITHUB_ID=
AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET=

# ── Made for you the first time ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Left empty, the first `docker compose up` makes these and keeps them in your Convex deployment's settings. Set them
# only to bring keys from another copy. MASTER_KEY_V1 encrypts the API keys people save in CareerBot; losing it means
# entering those again.
JWT_PRIVATE_KEY=
JWKS=
MASTER_KEY_V1=

# ── Optional ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Choosing a Google Drive folder in Settings (a browser API key for Google's Picker).
GOOGLE_PICKER_KEY=
# Liquid AI's model, for an experiment in sorting roles.
LIQUIDAI_API_KEY=
# Once a day this copy asks https://careerbot.dev/releases.json whether there's a newer version, and its owner sees it
# in Settings, Updates. Nothing about you or this copy is sent, but like any web request it shows careerbot.dev this
# server's IP address. off: never ask (the owner can also turn it off there).
# UPDATE_CHECK=off
# Ports on this computer, if the defaults are taken. Change SITE_URL, NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL and CONVEX_SITE_ORIGIN to match
# when they're localhost addresses (behind a domain they stay as they are). Dokploy's own dashboard is on 3000: there,
# set WEB_PORT=3001 or another free port.
# WEB_PORT=3000
# CONVEX_PORT=3210
# CONVEX_SITE_PORT=3211
# CONVEX_DASHBOARD_PORT=6791
# The address those ports are published on. 127.0.0.1 (the default): this machine only; Coolify's and Dokploy's proxies
# don't need them, and on a server they'd skip the proxy and its HTTPS (Docker's ports also go around ufw). Set 0.0.0.0
# only to reach the ports from other machines without a proxy, never on a server facing the internet.
# BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
